---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { ABOUT, unfilled, inWords } from "../lib/about";
import { REGISTERS, plannedRegisters } from "../lib/registers";

// Unfilled facts render an unmissable draft banner rather than quietly shipping
// as "«founder's full name»". src/lib/about.ts also exports validateAbout(),
// which throws instead — swap it in here once the four values are filled, so a
// later edit that blanks one cannot reach a live page.
const missing = unfilled();

const commitments = [
  "No political party affiliation, membership or endorsement.",
  "No government funding. If that ever changes, it will be disclosed here before the money is accepted.",
  "No paid advocacy, consulting or lobbying on any matter this site covers.",
  "No commercial products, sponsorships or advertising.",
  "Every dataset published in full under a named open licence.",
  "Every method published, including what it cannot see.",
  "A corrections policy with a named contact and a stated turnaround.",
];
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who publishes The Too Hard Basket, who runs it, how it is funded, and every connection that could bear on its independence."
>
  <section class="wrap intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Who publishes this, and why you can check</h1>
    <p class="lede measure">
      This site keeps a record of obligations governments owe to parliaments: reports
      answered, reports not answered, and how long it took. A record like that is only
      worth as much as the confidence you can place in whoever keeps it. So this page sets
      out who that is, how it is paid for, and every connection that could reasonably bear
      on it — including the one that gave the project its name.
    </p>
  </section>

  {missing.length > 0 && (
    <section class="wrap">
      <p class="draft measure">
        <strong>Draft — not for publication.</strong> This page still contains unfilled
        placeholders: {missing.join(", ")}. Fill them in <code>src/lib/about.ts</code>.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>The publisher</h2>
    <p>
      The Too Hard Basket is published by
      {" "}<strong><a href={ABOUT.site}>{ABOUT.entityName}</a></strong> (APIA),
      an incorporated association registered in {ABOUT.state} under the {ABOUT.act},
      incorporation number {ABOUT.incorporationNumber}, ABN {ABOUT.abn}.
      {" "}{ABOUT.acncRegistered ? "APIA is registered with the ACNC." : "APIA is not registered with the ACNC"}
      {ABOUT.dgrEndorsed
        ? " and is endorsed as a deductible gift recipient."
        : " and is not endorsed as a deductible gift recipient, so donations to it are not tax deductible."}
    </p>
    <p>
      APIA exists to publish and maintain independent datasets about Australian government
      performance. The emphasis is on <em>maintain</em>. Public-interest projects often begin
      with strong intent and then stop being updated, and a register that goes stale is worse
      than no register, because people go on citing it. Putting the dataset under an
      incorporated body rather than a personal account is an attempt to make it outlive the
      enthusiasm of the person who started it.
    </p>
    <p>
      APIA is governed by a committee of {inWords(ABOUT.committeeSize)}, of which
      {" "}{ABOUT.founderName} is President.
      {ABOUT.committeeNames.length > 0
        ? ` The committee is ${ABOUT.committeeNames.join(", ")}.`
        : " The membership is on the public register maintained by Consumer Protection (WA)."}
      {" "}{ABOUT.fundingStatement} If that changes, this page will say so and name the source.
      {" "}<a href={ABOUT.governancePage}>APIA's governance page</a> carries the constitution,
      the legal status and the same disclosures.
    </p>
    {ABOUT.soloOperated && (
      <>
        <p>
          <strong>In practice this is one person's work.</strong> The other two committee
          members provide the governance an incorporated association requires; they do not
          direct the project or review it day to day. That is worth saying plainly, because
          “a committee of {inWords(ABOUT.committeeSize)}” implies more institutional weight
          than exists, and a reader who worked it out later would be entitled to wonder what
          else had been oversold.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also matters less than it might. Nothing here asks you to trust the organisation.
          Every figure is computed by published code from published data, every row names and
          links the official document it came from, and the {" "}<a href="/methods">methods page</a>{" "}
          sets out what the method cannot see as well as what it can. If something on this
          site is wrong, it is wrong in a way you can demonstrate — which is a better
          guarantee than a larger committee would be.
        </p>
      </>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>Disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>{ABOUT.founderName}, {ABOUT.founderRole}, volunteers in an unpaid capacity in
      the electorate office of Kate Chaney MP, the independent member for Curtin.</strong>
      He is not employed by her, not paid by her, and not directed by her.
      APIA receives no funding, direction or editorial input from any parliamentarian, party
      or candidate, and this site was not requested by any of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is a second half to that disclosure, and it is the more important one, because it
      concerns where the project came from. The idea for this register — and the phrase
      “too hard basket” — came from watching Kate Chaney press the government about House
      committee reports it had never answered. Several were answered afterwards. The
      observation that followed was not about her: it was that a list of unanswered reports
      only existed because one member happened to compile it, that it covered one chamber,
      and that it stopped being maintained when the political moment passed. This site is an
      attempt to keep that list permanently, for every chamber, so that making it does not
      depend on anyone's attention.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      A disclosure volunteered reads as integrity. The identical fact discovered later reads
      as concealment. That is the whole reason this section is near the top of the page rather
      than in a footnote.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>Independence, as commitments rather than adjectives</h2>
    <p>
      “Independent” is a word anyone can print. These are the specific things it means here,
      and each of them is checkable or falsifiable:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {commitments.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      It costs very little to run — a domain, a mailbox and a static host — and that is
      deliberate: a register that needs money to survive eventually needs someone who has
      money to like it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
    <p>
      It does not say whether a committee's recommendation was a good one, or whether the
      government was right to accept or reject it. Those are arguments, and there are plenty
      of people having them. This site records only what the official record shows: that a
      report was tabled, that a response was or was not tabled, and when.
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not attribute motive. Where a response is absent, this site says no response is
      recorded — not that one was refused, suppressed or avoided. Where a judgment is finely
      balanced, it is resolved in the government's favour, so that every figure here is a floor
      rather than a ceiling.
    </p>
    <p>
      And it is not partisan, which is easier to demonstrate than to assert: the
      {" "}<a href="/senate/#by-government">record by government</a> shows four governments of
      both persuasions inside the same narrow band, published under both of the two defensible
      ways of attributing a late response, so that neither can be quoted alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If something here is wrong, it should be fixed, and the fastest way to make that happen
      is to say so. Write to
      {" "}<a href="mailto:corrections@toohardbasket.org.au">corrections@toohardbasket.org.au</a>{" "}
      with the row and the source. Corrections are made within 24 hours of being confirmed and
      listed in the {" "}<a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>, so the record of what
      this site said, and when it changed, stays visible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anything else: <a href="mailto:contact@toohardbasket.org.au">contact@toohardbasket.org.au</a>.
      The dataset and the code that produces every figure are in the
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket">public repository</a> —
      data under CC BY 4.0, code under the MIT licence.
      {" "}<a href="/methods">How the register is built</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>What comes next</h2>
    <p>
      The Senate was the first register and the House of Representatives is the second.
      {" "}{plannedRegisters().length} more {plannedRegisters().length === 1 ? "is" : "are"} planned —
      {" "}{plannedRegisters().map((r) => r.name).join("; ")} —
      and each is published as its own register with its own rule rather than merged into
      the others, because the obligation is different in each case and averaging them would
      hide that. The Senate asks for three months and the House for six; a single combined
      number would mean nothing.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Registers live: {REGISTERS.filter((r) => r.status === "live").length}.
      Planned: {plannedRegisters().length}.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro { padding-bottom: 1rem; }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.75rem; }
  .note { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted); }
  ul { padding-left: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  code { font-size: 0.875em; }
  .draft {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-left: 3px solid var(--series-1);
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; background: var(--surface-sunken);
  }
</style>
